  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1039 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nAs we get into the start of next week, we will remain dry. However, \nunder semi-zonal flow aloft, afternoon highs will climb a degree or \ntwo above normal values as early as Monday and even another degree \nor two warmer on Tuesday. Per usual, a warmup like this is ahead of \nour next potential weather maker. Here's the catch: models aren't in \ngreat agreement on what that next weather maker looks like. The most \nrecent NBM does jump PoPs starting Wednesday evening (40-50% up from \n20%), but the GFS and Euro that make up a large portion of the NBM \nlook very different. The GFS shows a much more amplified upper level \ntrough over the mid-Mississippi Valley bringing showers and maybe \neven a few storms to the mid-state Wednesday night through Thursday \nas surface low pressure is developed much closer to home. The 00Z \nEuro is starkly different with a much less amplified upper trough \nand a surface low up in Ohio. While the PoPs may be the same, those \noutcomes would look totally different. In the end, look for \nincreased rain chances mid-week and another shot of cooler air to \narrive in time for next weekend -- maybe even widespread 20s for \nmorning lows on Saturday.",
